\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



The four hyperedges are distinct. Each of the two pairs must be disjoint, and their unions must agree. For fixed uniformity $t$, the proposed asymptotic is a threshold in the number of distinct edges as the vertex count grows.

An $r$-uniform hypergraph is a family of distinct $r$-element subsets of a vertex set. A proper vertex colouring has no monochromatic edge. A transversal is a vertex set meeting every edge; its minimum size is the covering number. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/643}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $f(n;t)$ be minimal such that if a $t$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices contains at least $f(n;t)$ edges then there must be four edges $A,B,C,D$ such that $ A\cup B= C\cup D $ and $ A\cap B=C\cap D=\emptyset. $ Estimate $f(n;t)$ - in particular, is it true that for $t\geq 3$ $ f(n;t)=(1+o(1))\binom{n}{t-1}? $ \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Prove asymptotic constant 1 for each fixed t>=3 and establish limit existence for t>=4. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

